      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let's investigate the width of the subset lattice.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For <m>n=1,2,3,4</m>, find the width <m>w</m> of <m>\mbf{2}^{n}</m> and a partition into <m>w</m> chains.
            </p>
              <tabular>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center"> </col>
                <row bottom="minor">
                  <cell> <m>n</m> </cell>
                  <cell>1</cell>
                  <cell>2</cell>
                  <cell>3</cell>
                  <cell>4</cell>
                  <cell>5</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell> <m>\width(\mathbf{2}^{n})</m> </cell>
                  <cell><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/> </cell>
                  <cell><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/> </cell>
                  <cell><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/> </cell>
                  <cell><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/> </cell>
                  <cell><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/><nbsp/> </cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            
          </statement>
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <tabular>
              <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center"> </col>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell> <m>n</m> </cell>
                <cell>1</cell>
                <cell>2</cell>
                <cell>3</cell>
                <cell>4</cell>
              </row>
              
              <row>
                <cell> <m>\width(\mathbf{2}^{n})</m> </cell>
                <cell>1</cell>
                <cell>2</cell>
                <cell>3</cell>
                <cell>6</cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.25cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Develop a conjecture about the width of <m>\mbf{2}^{n}</m> for general <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              It looks like the width of <m>\mbf{2}^{n}</m> is the largest binomial coefficient <m>C(n,k)</m>, and it appears this happens when <m>k</m> is around <m>n/2</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            To be able to verify our conjecture, let’s think about a seemingly unrelated question: counting maximal chains in <m>\mbf{2}^{n}</m>. How many maximal chains are there in <m>\mbf{2}^{n}</m>? <em>Hint</em>: Start with <m>n=1,2,3</m>.
              Think about the number of choices of the “next” set when forming a maximal chain.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <!-- <hint>
            <p>
              Start with <m>n=1,2,3</m>.
              Think about the number of choices of the “next” set when forming a maximal chain.
            </p>
          </hint> -->
        
          
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              There are <m>n!</m> maximal chains, since you can start from the empty set and add elements one-by-one in <m>n!</m> orders until you get to <m>[n]</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <introduction>
            <p>
              Now let’s think about maximal chains through particular sets.
              Look at <m>\mbf{2}^{5}</m>.
            </p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
              <p>
                How many maximal chains pass through the set <m>\{2,4\}</m>?
              </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
              <p>
                <m>2!3! = 12</m> since we have <m>2!</m> in which to add <m>2</m> and <m>4</m> and then <m>3!</m> orders in which to add the remaining three elements.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
              <p>
                How many maximal chains pass through the set <m>\{1,3,4\}</m>?
              </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
              <p>
                Here we have <m>3!</m> ways to add the elements <m>1,3,4</m> and then <m>2!</m> ways to add <m>2</m> and <m>5</m>.
                Thus, there are <m>3!2!</m> maximal chains through this set.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>
        </exercise>
          <exercise>
            <introduction>
              <p>
                Now think about <m>\mbf{2}^{n}</m>.
              </p>
            </introduction>
            <task workspace="0.5in">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  How many maximal chains pass through the set <m>\{2,4\}</m>?
                </p>
              </statement>
              <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
                <p>
                  <m>2!(n-2)!</m> since we have <m>2!</m> in which to add <m>2</m> and <m>4</m> and then <m>(n-2)!</m> orders in which to add the remaining <m>n-2</m> elements.
                </p>
              </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="0.5in">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  How many maximal chains pass through the set <m>\{1,3,4\}</m>?
                </p>
              </statement>
              <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
                <p>
                  Here we have <m>3!</m> ways to add the elements <m>1,3,4</m> and then <m>(n-3)!</m> ways to add the remaining <m>n-3</m> elements.
                  Thus, there are <m>3!(n-3)!</m> maximal chains through this set.
                </p>
              </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="0.5in">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  How many maximal chains pass through a set <m>S</m> with <m>|S|=k</m>?
                </p>
              </statement>
              <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
                <p>
                  Here we have <m>k!</m> ways to add the <m>k</m> elements that are in the set and then <m>(n-k)!</m> ways to add the remaining <m>n-k</m> elements.
                  Thus, there are <m>k!(n-k)!</m> maximal chains through this set.
                </p>
              </solution>
            </task>
          </exercise>
        
        
      <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Now let’s see how we can use counting maximal chains through a set to get an upper bound on <m>\width(\mbf{2}^{n})</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
              Fix an antichain <m>\mc{A}=\{S_{1},S_{2},\dots,S_{t}\}</m> with <m>|S_{i}|=s_{i}</m>.
              How many maximal chains pass through <em>some</em> set in <m>\mc{A}</m>? <em>Hint</em>: Express your answer as a summation.
            </p>
        </statement>
          <!-- <hint>
            <p>
              Express your answer as a summation.
            </p>
          </hint> -->
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              We know that the number of maximal chains passing through <m>S_{i}</m> is <m>s_{i}!(n-s_{i})!</m>, so then we add up and get
              <me>
                \sum_{i=1}^{t} s_{i}!(n-s_{i})!.
              </me>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Can a maximal chain <m>\mc{C}</m> pass through two different sets <m>S_{i},S_{j}\in\mc{A}</m> with <m>i\neq j</m>? What does this tell you that the sum from the previous question must be less than or equal to?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              <m>n!</m>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              As a result of the previous step, you should have an inequality with a summation on the small side and a factorial on the big side.
              Divide the factorial over (and into the summation) so that you have a summation less than or equal to <m>1</m>.
              Rewrite your summation so that it is a sum of reciprocals of binomial coefficients.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              <me>
                \sum_{i=1}^{t} \frac{1}{\binom{n}{s_i}}\leq 1
              </me>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              We will establish a formula for <m>w=\width(\mbf{2}^{n})</m> by showing that <m>w</m> is bounded above and below by the same quantity.
              Explain why <m>w\geq C(n,k)</m> for each possible value of <m>k</m>.
              What is the largest lower bound that you get for <m>w</m> in this way?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              Since the <m>k</m>-element subsets of <m>[n]</m> each form an antichain and there are <m>C(n,k)</m> <m>k</m>-element subsets of <m>[n]</m>, we know that <m>w\geq C(n,k)</m> for all <m>k</m>.
              The largest possible bound is thus the largest binomial coefficient, which happens when <m>k=\lceil n/2\rceil</m>.
              (Because of the symmetry of binomial coefficients, when <m>n</m> is odd, we have the same value if <m>k = \lfloor n/2\rfloor</m>.)
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let us now return to the inequality involving a summation and <m>1</m> that you obtained earlier.
              To find an upper bound for <m>w</m>, we assume that <m>\mc{A}</m> is a maximum antichain so that <m>t = w=\width(\mbf{2}^{n})</m>.
              Also assume that <m>s_{i} = \lceil n/2\rceil</m> for all <m>i</m>.
              We can do this since the inequality is true for any antichain.
              Explain how this lets you eliminate the summation from your inequality and then give an upper bound for <m>\width(\mbf{2}^{n})</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution component="sublat-width-soln">
            <p>
              When all the terms have the same value, the inequality can be rewritten as
              <me>
                \frac{w}{\binom{n}{\left\lceil \frac{n}{2}\right\rceil}}\leq 1,
              </me>
              which implies <m>w\leq \binom{n}{\lceil n/2\rceil}</m>.
              We now have matching upper and lower bounds for the width, since we know there is an antichain with <m>\binom{n}{\lceil n/2\rceil}</m> elements.
              Thus, this is the width.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    <!-- </page> -->
     
  </worksheet>
</chapter>